Here we have a classic example of a double standard. First, I take
considerable exception to the (implied) characterisation of rape threats
as "rudeness". Where I live, threatening rape violates the criminal
code.

But the double standard involves the matter of the seriousness of the
threat. In the instance of the request by the administrator, a student
can leap up, read the free speech guarantees at the university (and,
in many places, in the national constitution as well) and inform the 
administrator that if the "request" becomes a disciplinary issue, the
student will solicit the aid of the local civil liberties association.

So, in that sense, the student can "ignore", and even defy, the "censor".
And I suspect that in 99%+ of cases like that, at least in the US and 
Canada, the administrator concerned will back down. Even if they don't,
the university can, at the very worst, mildly disrupt your education
with a suspension. I don't believe any administration today would risk
an expulsion (except at West Point); if you have a counter-example, again,
please post it. 

