In article hcf@senator-bedfellow.MIT.EDU, leb@athena.mit.edu (LARS E BADER) writes:

> "Get raped with a big dick" is NOT a threat.  It is purely an insult -- and
> since it makes no statement about the recipient, it is actually not as
> hurtful as a good many other insults.  Regardless of how often a member of
> category X is subjected to some unpleasant behavior, another person's
> statement that they wish such a behavior would be visited upon that person 
> by some unspecified third party is not a threat.  Only a moron could think so.
>
> ("I will rape you" IS a threat.  But it is not a very credible one, coming
> from a distant stranger who has no idea where you live or what you look like,
> and when there is no evidence that such a Usenet threat has ever been acted 
> out.  I doubt many such threats are made on Usenet in any event.)

Well, obviously I don't have the intelligence to deal with this (since you
describe me as a moron), so I'll have to fall back on logic. 

If we set specific semantic rules for "threats", such as a requirement
that the word "I" appear in the text of the alleged threat, what follows?
It becomes fairly easy to make statements that an ordinary, intelligent
person would regard as indicating danger from the source. It would become
acceptable, for example, to sit in the bank loan office, and say (while 
opening your coat to show some cylindrical objects under your shirt) that
you bet a few frustrated applicants come in with dynamite under their shirts,
and a crazy person could sure bring this place down in a hurry. And similar
unfoccussed statements and actions that avoid the word "I", but indicate an
intention with clarity.

So let us try another analogy. Let us make "category X" the set of all
small businesses in a row that a (shady) developer wants to tear down and
make into office towers. And let us say that three business people in
category X have had their businesses burned out. In that circumstances,
would an off-hand remark like: I hope stubborn idiots like you get burned
out from an agent of the developer qualify as a threat? Maybe not according
to you, but it would certainly make me consider moving.

> As for relative rates of assault, no one really knows.  There are hundreds
> of thousands of men in prisons and if 20% were raped 50 times per year
> by the thugs who control them (entirely possible given the violentness of 
> prisons), they would make up a considerable share of all assault victims.  
> But this isn't really relevant, anymore than the rate at which women are 
> raped is relevant to relabelling an insult a threat.

Actually, the "get raped" line qualifies (technically) as neither an insult
nor a threat, but as a malediction. But it has the character of a threat,
because the recipient has good reason to assume that the sender could (at
least in theory) make the curse come true. 

As for the "relative" rates of assault: quite right, they do not have any
relevance. Only the absolute rates matter. The question comes down to: do
women get raped in sufficient numbers to make any woman take this threat
seriously? (I would suggest they do. So would the FBI, the RCMP, and my 
local police force). Do feminists or other women who speak up regularly
get raped to shut them up? That question doesn't admit much of an answer,
since few rapists characterise their rapes as "political" during the act.
Women who speak up do get threatened with rape, and with death, and with
having their children murdered, and so on, and some women do get raped, and
murdered, and have their children murdered.

So it becomes easier (and safer) to shut up in that situation. And then
we don't get the information and support that women could (potentially)
give us on the net, if threats (oops, maledictions) didn't somehow worry
them.

 
