In article 6ee@senator-bedfellow.MIT.EDU, sethf@athena.mit.edu (Seth Finkelstein) writes:

[ about disciplinary action for turning down "requests" ]
 
> 	In general (not yet in direct reply), do you agree:
> 
> 	1) Such provisions exist?  and
> 	2) Administrators would use them?

1) probably, if you read the fine print in some university code of conduct
   it will say something about complying with reasonable requests, etc.

2) I can't imagine it happening over a free speech issue. I can see 
   administrators taking action over these issues, but not without a full
   debate.

> 	So is your lack of belief restricted only to making it "stick"?

Well, that not only relates to the credibility of the "threat" in question,
it also relates to the question of whether any threat at all existed. 

If an administrator had said "do as I say or I'll have you off the system, 
with no recourse, then you'd have a good case to start with.
But instead, you have a polite (and entirely reasonable) request that you
suggest some student might construe as a "threat". So you have to make the
case the the administrator who made the request could have backed it up
with disciplinary action, and the student knew that possibility existed.

Unless you can show me a reason the student might expect disciplinary
action under the circumstances, and some evidence that such discipline
might actually inconvenience the student, then I suggest you have no case
for a "threat" of any kind. 

> I take it from the above that you are holding to the fallacy that no-one
> in power would do anything contrary to 'common sense', common sense of course
> being defined as what you think is right. Also, consider that pedants
> and martinets can reasonably expected to be found in judging positions.

Kindly don't put words in my mouth. People in power often act contrary to
common sense; however, they generally set limits on their courting of
disaster. 

> 	To get back to specifics, all of the above, and your objection,
> is irrelevant to my main point. The greatest harm is not the the charge
> will stick (though that is bad enough). Indeed, in the cases I know
> where people have fought arbitrary prohibitions, at tremendous cost of
> time and money, they tend to win. Rather, it is the massive chilling
> effect caused by the intimidation of people who do not have the
> resources to effectively fight back that is the driving force of repression.
> 	People rarely get convicted yes, but they very often get intimidated.

Ok, then, we get back full circle. You admit that no credible threat exists
(that people who fight back "tend to win"). You can't produce a single
example of a code of conduct that would make your perception of "threat" valid.

Look, I don't defend the abuse of power. And yes, university administrators do
abuse their power (although they come down on undergrads a lot less frequently
than you seem to think). But the discussion to which I refer concerned what
the person concerned described as a polite request. And nobody came up with 
any evidence to show that she made anything but a polite request. And you
admit, above, that if a student refused to accede to her request, the
student would probably win.

And you still insist on seeing a threat here? Come on! 

> 	Absolutely. I have a double standard. I'm a lot more concerned
> about the actions of people in official power than the flamings of net.nuts.

So you create hypersensitive criteria that makes virtually any remark or
other action of an administrator "in power" a "threat" (if they ask a 
student to "pass the salt", according to the disciplinary code you suggest
without citing, that request contains a "threat"). 

> > Where I live, threatening rape violates the criminal code.
> 
> 	I suspect this is limited to a "credible" or "true" threat, and
> a message from someone a long way away, with no other evidence giving
> giving the recipient cause to believe it would be carried out would not
> qualify for prosecution (tack on "no matter how much it indicates the sender
> is the scum of the earth who deserves to rot in hell forever" if you want).

Fortunately, I live in Canada, where the relevant offence lies in allowing
the words to pass your lips (or your keyboard).

But again, the threat contained in an outright declaration of intention
to cause harm has, ipso facto, far more "credibility" than the "threat"
contained in a polite request. 

> 	Yes. But your definition of violence seems rather expansive,
> including just words by a distant person with *no* *other* *SPECIFIC*
> *reason* to believe they will be acted upon (this latter clause is a
> crucial part, please consider it carefully before replying).
> 	So rephase the above from "bad people" to "saying bad things".

You keep insisting on "distant". You have no reason to believe that
distance exists (other than the way "distance" involves a superficial
lessening of the threat). Internet extends to the next room, as well
as the next continent.

[ a threat that shuts someone up amounts to censorship no matter ]
[ who utters it.                                                 ]

> 	Gee, I thought that's what I was trying to get across in the
> above. But you seem to think that as long as the threat is in good
> cause (for standards, anti-rape, whatever), and 'polite', it deserves
> no negative comment.

Garbage! I never said anything of the kind!

I said and maintain that in the case of the administrator's request	
(note that the word "request" and "threat" do not mean the same thing)
nobody came up with a single shred of evidence that the request involved
anything even approaching a threat.

Since you seem to need this spelled out:

1) I do consider administrators capable of making threats, just like
   anyone else.

2) I do not consider any threat excusable.

3) I (now this involves a somewhat tricky concept, so read carefully)
   consider the nature of the threat lies, not in the interpretations
   possible to the person receiving the communication, not in the 
   situation of the person making the communication, but in the
   intentions communicated. 

   In other words, a polite request, even from someone with a gun
   and a license to use it, does not constitute a threat. Conversely,
   if someone in the deepest dungeon, locked up for life, screams that
   they plan to kill me, that does constitute a threat.

   Otherwise, you land up with a situation where decision makers
   literally can not open their mouths without committing a crime. 

[ irrelevant argument about totalitarian states deleted ]

> 	Now, suppose I say that the above lines have caused me to think
> you are dangerously mentally unbalanced, and *if* you are psychotic the
> consequences of ignoring you could be severe. I argue that it would be
> good idea to err on the safe side, and have the authorities take an
> interest in you. Remember, you aren't the one who will make the decision
> as to the credibility of the charge. Do you really believe that it aids
> freedom to have such things going on?

Oh, don't worry. Every time I point out that threats involve the use
(in the strict sense of the term, as in to employ, to turn to account)
of violence, someone tells me they plan to treat me as a dangerous
lunatic. I have got used to it by now. 

So go ahead. You want the address of my postmaster? How about my boss?
Sorry, they already know about my crazy habit of treating threats against
people as, well, something wrong.

