1) There is no thing called a "First Amendment" in -this- country
(Canada).  Usenet is trans-national -- which makes any sort of legal-based
restrictions a bit difficult to manage. The best one can likely hope for
is some sort of moral restrictions more restrictive than the First Amendment
of the Constitution of The United States of America. I seeing nothing 'nuts'
about hoping that such moral restrictions might come about.


2) The First Amendment of the USA has been interpreted by the US courts
several times, and been found not to be open-ended. Roughly speaking,
it has been found that only private speech and speech in public
places is protected. Public speech in non-public places is not
protected. The computer networks are not public places in the terms
of the courts of the USA. It is, then, not illegal to restrict what
may be said via Usenet in the USA.

Remember, that though you in the USA may have a 'right' to say
pretty well whatever you like, your rights do not extend to forcing
any entity to carry your message; nor do your rights extend to forcing
any entity to do business with you [except perhaps a few government
departments.] If your immediate upstream site decided to strip all
your messages out of the feed before they got any further, you would
have no recourse except to find a different upstream site. [Unless
that is your contract with them specifically prohibitted them from
doing that stripping, in which case you would have a simple breach-of-
contract suit on which the First Amendment had no bearing.]

Ruth was quite right to put "so-called" before "freedom of speech"
in that context, because there is no right or freedom in US law
to perform the actions she refers to -- not when it comes to Usenet.


3) Many posters in the USA have agreed to the NSFNet Acceptable Usage
Policy (AUP), which forbids postings such as Ruth refers to. The AUP
is a voluntary agreement by which in return for agreeing not to do some
things, one gains access through NSFNet-funded equipment to the internet.
In theory, the AUP is the type of 'guarantee' that Karl claims does not
exist. Unfortunately, in practice, the AUP seems to be rather selectively
enforced :-(  It would appear that in the future, the AUP is going
to become less and less relevant, as the internet is "privatized".
Unfortunate, that.

