OK, for those who flamed the shit out of me for everything from daring
to teach philosophy with "my" attitudes/ideas, to promoting censorship,
or PC fascism or whatever else you chose to find fault with:

Let me rephrase the question:

Do you believe that appropriate net useage includes being able to post
anything one might possibly type, anywhere, to any number of newsgroups,
regardless of its relevance to the newsgroup topic(s) or charter(s)?

Contrary to your worst assumptions about me, I do not wish either to
prevent Mr. Zeleny from posting articles such as "Hannah Arendt's
Wrinkled Cunt," or to prevent my students from encountering words or
ideas that they (or others) might consider offensive.  Indeed, I support
the availability of robust speech in the critique of ideas.

However, it is my impression that the Usenet community only is able to
function at all because of the voluntary (or sometimes slightly coerced)
acceptance of some common practice guidelines.  If anything whatsoever
could reasonable be posted anywhere (or EVERYwhere), there might as well
be only one Usenet newsgroup, talk.everything.  Presumably, Usenet
guidelines specify that Mr. Zeleny ought only post "Hannah Arendt's
Wrinkled Cunt" to the Usenet group (POSSIBLY crossposted to one or two
other groups, if it is of broad general interest) in which it is most
applicable to the discussion.  It is not, believe me, such a brilliant
contribution to philosophy that it deserves crossposting to all the
Usenet philosophy groups, each of which has a different philosophical
focus.

I have been reading (and ignoring) Mr. Zeleny's posts to the philosophy
newsgroups for years.  It is not the fact that he posts offensive
material to which I objected when I wrote to his sysadmin and posted to
this newsgroup.  It is the fact that he crossposts it so widely (i.e.,
to every single philosophy newsgroup, adding more & more to his list of
crossposts every time another group is created) that his crossposting is
impossible to escape.  Are the members of this newsgroup defending THIS
as appropriate use of Usenet resources?

