I think that very few of us would actually _defend_ what you describe 
above.  It is in fact a general principle of Usenet etiquette that one
should crosspost sparingly if at all, and only to "relevant" newsgroups.

The problem is in enforcement.  Basically there are only four legitimate
courses of action against people like Zelezny:  (a) flame him, preferably
via e-mail; (b) complain to the administrators of the system he's posting
from; (c) create a moderated newsgroup which can exclude postings like his;
(d) ignore him.

Unfortunately, (a) often has little effect, as people like Zelezny seem to
be born with asbestos suits.  (b) may or not be effective, depending on 
how extreme the offenses are.  (c) requires a volunteer to moderate the
group, and a full-blown "election" to create it.  

In the end, we usually have to stick with (d), and warn newcomers to Usenet
that they must be prepared to do likewise.  In your case, I would explain 
carefully to your students the sort of things they can expect to run 
across, explain the tools that they can use to bypass those things,
and finally, allow the really thin-skinned people to opt out of this
particular assignment.  Or maybe you could suggest some mailing lists,
since those tend to be a bit more "orderly", in my experience.

I sympathize with you.  We're about to open up our Internet/Usenet connection
to the general campus public.  Even without the 'alt' groups, I'm sure
there's plenty of material on our disk that would upset some students,
parents, and trustees.

