There is in the United States, which is where Ginsberg posted from.  And I,
frankly, do not hope for a "moral restriction" on the content of the Usenet.
I further contend that those PC people who wish to see "moral" activity of
this sort are not moral themselves in their desire to restrict freedom of
expression.

>2) The First Amendment of the USA has been interpreted by the US courts
>several times, and been found not to be open-ended. Roughly speaking,
>it has been found that only private speech and speech in public
>places is protected. Public speech in non-public places is not
>protected. The computer networks are not public places in the terms
>of the courts of the USA. It is, then, not illegal to restrict what
>may be said via Usenet in the USA.

They are in many cases.  Much of the network is publically funded by the US
taxpayer.  That may constitute a public fora.  This has not yet been tested
in the courts of this country, but I bet I can make just as cogent an
argument that it is indeed a public forum as you can that it is not.

Certainly, any <individual> system may not be a public forum.  That is, you
are free to restrict what <you> accept or forward.  However, the NSF backbone
is certainly funded with public dollars, and thus, like the sidewalk, is a
public forum.  

If I contract with a common-carrier style Usenet site and start posting, you
have no way to stop me under the laws of the United States.  This is, IMHO,
a good thing.

>Remember, that though you in the USA may have a 'right' to say
>pretty well whatever you like, your rights do not extend to forcing
>any entity to carry your message; nor do your rights extend to forcing
>any entity to do business with you [except perhaps a few government
>departments.] If your immediate upstream site decided to strip all
>your messages out of the feed before they got any further, you would
>have no recourse except to find a different upstream site. [Unless
>that is your contract with them specifically prohibitted them from
>doing that stripping, in which case you would have a simple breach-of-
>contract suit on which the First Amendment had no bearing.]

Yep.  However, if you contract with a company that is trying to be a 
common carrier then that company is on very shaky legal ground if they 
start censoring what you have to say.  There are reasons that common 
carrier-style companies (UUNET is an example) aren't troubled by the 
content in alt.binaries.pictures.erotica -- they don't attempt to censor 
the material, and act like common carriers to a large extent.

If UUNET (as an example) was to start censoring people's expression, or 
anyone else does, they had better not carry any of the other controversial 
groups such as a.b.p.e.  If they do so some wisecracker is going to get the 
idea that they are fertile ground for retaliation -- and expose them to 
a copyright infringement lawsuit the likes of which the Gods have never 
seen before.  Consider the content of a.b.p.e and then tell me, at $100k 
per infringement, how much money you think this would cost that company?

You see, if you censor based on content you aren't a common carrier any 
longer.  This is a problem for those who would prefer the "hands off" 
approach to news propagation -- which is in most of these folks, and 
probably your, best interest.  For when you take out the scissors of 
censorship you find that you are suddenly held to a higher standard -- if
you can take the time and trouble to filter out "offensive" postings, you
certainly can take the time to filter out illegal ones!

Note that in "Cubby .v. Compuserve" this was <exactly> what was held --
since CompuServe exerts no prior restraint on speech, and in fact does 
not attempt to censor information, they were not liable for the posted
defamatory information which got them sued.  Since this is now an
established piece of case law in the US, it is <very> relavent to the
discussion.

You can probably get away with not carrying an entire message stream
(newsgroup) without exposing yourself.  However, when you censor on <content>
instead of the message stream itself, you are in serious trouble with the 
common carrier analog and therefore expose yourself to liability for what 
you <do> have in your news spool.

>Ruth was quite right to put "so-called" before "freedom of speech"
>in that context, because there is no right or freedom in US law
>to perform the actions she refers to -- not when it comes to Usenet.

Balderdash.  There is such a freedom <if a university or company respects
it>.  Universities may not have a choice, especially if they receive public
funding (see recent examples where schools had to allow Neo-Nazis to march
for an object lesson in what our Constitution can mean to those who are
public entities).  Companies are certainly free to embrace the principle of
freedom of expression.  MCSNet, my company, certainly does.  This is not to
say that there are no limits.  There are.  The First Amendment does not
give license to slander or otherwise illegal activity.  It does absolutely 
protect "offensive" speech, and particularly protects politically oriented 
offensive speech.

Wesleyan is a private school from what I understand.  Therefore, <they> are
probably not held to this standard.  However, that does not entitle them to
force their opinion on others.

>3) Many posters in the USA have agreed to the NSFNet Acceptable Usage
>Policy (AUP), which forbids postings such as Ruth refers to. The AUP
>is a voluntary agreement by which in return for agreeing not to do some
>things, one gains access through NSFNet-funded equipment to the internet.
>In theory, the AUP is the type of 'guarantee' that Karl claims does not
>exist. Unfortunately, in practice, the AUP seems to be rather selectively
>enforced :-(  It would appear that in the future, the AUP is going
>to become less and less relevant, as the internet is "privatized".
>Unfortunate, that.

Unfortunate?  I disagree.

I believe it is <very> fortunate -- for the Universities connected to AUP
restricted networks.

I, as an example, am connected to a commercial part of the Internet.  I 
can therefore post anything I want (within the bounds of US law) to the 
network since I have no direct connections over AUP-sponsored links.  If 
someone takes a message of mine via an AUP link, it is <their> responsibility,
not mine.  THEY solicited that information by obtaining the group(s) in
question and sending the "OK" reply to the "IHAVE" NNTP message.  The result 
of a strict AUP enforcement effort would not be the banning of sites such as
mine -- I'm not subject to the AUP directly on Usenet -- it would rather be 
the banning of the sites which took the traffic, which would quickly render 
the AUP moot (an AUP with no remaining subscribers to the affected networks 
is rather useless, is it not?)

Under the AUP, ALL sites carrying Usenet are in technical violation.  There 
is no academic or research purpose served by the majority of the material on 
the net, and that is the test the AUP requires to be met.  Usenet could not
be argued to be "coincidental" traffic (one of the exceptions) as the volume
is just too large.  Certainly, alt.binaries.pictures.erotica would disqualify
you from AUP-correctness.  So would nearly all of rec.* much of soc.* and 
alt.*.  

We haven't even begun to touch on email to non-AUP sites asking for dates,
postings and responses to alt.personals, or other non-AUP complient traffic.

Fact is, the AUP is a joke.  Its there so that the political folks can whack
those who get seriously out of line, and nothing more.  Strict enforcement 
would lead to the destruction of the academic Internet in the US, and the 
folks in charge know this.  Strict enforcement would also require a violation 
of ECPA.  Steve Jackson Games showed that it <is> possible to win those 
lawsuits against the Government in this country.

Now certainly the AUP can be used to censor those individuals posting
<from AUP connected systems>.  However, the percentage of sites subject to
this is shrinking rapidly, which IMHO is a <good thing>.

Usenet is not defined by a transport medium or a set of so-called community
standards.  It is a collection of message streams.  Much of these streams
passes over <private> connection services, whether commercial Internet or
dial-up UUCP.  The person who solicits that message transmission is the one 
rightfully responsible for the PCness of that message within their own 
domain.  If you don't like this message stream, then modify your software 
to exclude the messages from posters you don't like (or based on anything 
else -- words in the message, etc) from your own personal message stream.
Coerce your feeds to install the same software.  That nobody is doing this 
is not relavent to the discussion right now.  The fact remains that it 
is <possible>, if you care enough about the content of what you carry.  
Just be aware that by becoming a censor you are approving that which you 
do not exclude -- and that can have legal ramifications which are most 
unwelcome for your organization.

The fact also remains that small-minded people who would silence free 
expression on the net have NO practical way to currently enforce their 
wishes on the rest of us.  This is, IMHO, as it should be.

The best cure for offensive speech is more speech (I got quoted on WLS Talk
Radio Chicago for this line on a recent call).

G'day.

