[...]
>Let me rephrase the question:
>
>Do you believe that appropriate net useage includes being able to post
>anything one might possibly type, anywhere, to any number of newsgroups,
>regardless of its relevance to the newsgroup topic(s) or charter(s)?

Ugh. Gosh, *that* sounds familiar! IMHO, the short answer is yes. This has
been discussed elsewhere (to death and back), and it basically comes down
to this: no one person, or even group of people, can make a proper
determination as to what is and isn't relevant for everyone else. The simple
fact of the matter is that the same posts you find offensive may be what
others consider interesting. The best thing to do is to allow everything,
and let each individual reader make his/her decisions as to what they find
proper or not for themselves.

>I have been reading (and ignoring) Mr. Zeleny's posts to the philosophy
>newsgroups for years.  It is not the fact that he posts offensive
>material to which I objected when I wrote to his sysadmin and posted to
>this newsgroup.  It is the fact that he crossposts it so widely (i.e.,
>to every single philosophy newsgroup, adding more & more to his list of
>crossposts every time another group is created) that his crossposting is
>impossible to escape.

It sounds like your case is almost the ideal case for a global KILLfile.
When it's only one poster doing this over and over, it's the easiest thing
in the world to filter him/her/it out so it won't bother you. Check the
documentation of your newsreader for more information on how to do this,
or pick up a copy of the KILLfile FAQ.

> Are the members of this newsgroup defending THIS
>as appropriate use of Usenet resources?

Yes, in the same way many would defend another's right to preach a different
religion or way of thought, even if it's viewpoint is in direct opposition 
with your own personal beliefs.

If you really wish to control what your students read, you may want to look
into establishing a local, moderated newsgroup as others have suggested.
Put all the disclaimers you like to make the lawyers happy, but please don't
underestimate your students. I'm sure they can handle it. Perhaps even
better than you. Let them know how to access the unfiltered/uncensored
newsgroups, and let them decide for themselves. After all, you are trying to
prepare them for the real world, right? Besides, some of your students may
already be aware of the net, and may be far more offended and insulted by your
assuming they cannot use their own judgement than by anything they might read
here. I know I would be...
