From caf-talk Caf Aug 16 21:18:40 1993
Newsgroups: alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk
From: betsys@cs.umb.edu (Elizabeth Schwartz)
Subject: Re: A little suggestion
Message-ID: <BETSYS.93Aug16201929@eris.cs.umb.edu>
Date: Tue, 17 Aug 1993 00:19:32 GMT


Theres' a lot of gray area between casual requests and demands.
Sometimes, if it isn't clear, either the system administrator has to 
clarify which is which, or the user has to ask. I can't imagine a
system where ABSOLUTELY EVERY  request is taken as a threat or an
order... I feel sorry for anyone living under a system where
authority is taken to that sort of totalitarian extreme.

  Requests from system staff cover a whole continuum. If I ask someone
to run a process  night when the system is less busy, or to limit use
of the laser printer to one copy of a resume, or to yield the 
X-terminal to students in Software Engineering, or to not play games
when the system is busy, I'm doing my job to help the system flow
smoothly. I'm voicing guidelines. At our shop, the user who
occasionally oversteps a guideline is given a polite request not to do
so. We do expect the user to follow these requests... and the users
can expect these requests to be reasonable. We also almost always are 
willing to explain our reasoning and discuss alternatives, 

Repeated, multiple overstepping might lead to more formal
requests... and I personally am always very clear about identifying
when I am making a formal request. This isn't that hard to do. 

  A request to put paper in the recycling bin instead of the trash
is clearly not going to get a user expelled from school for not
following it....in that case, we are asking the student to volunteer. 
A request to not eat pizza in the terminal room will be followed by
someone standing over the eatee until they take the pizza
box elsewhere. I dunno *what* you'd call that, formally!  A request to
not print enormous files is backed by the very real possibility that
we could run out of paper and out of money to buy more. I would
consider asking a person who repeatedly did that to buy us a new box
of paper, but I'm sure that isn't something that would stick in court!
It's just a fair and community-minded consequence.

  I don't beleive that it's possible to write down into law all
aspects of the "Golden Rule." I don't think we have to try. 

The University only has policies to cover very major infractions and
very serious problems, with very serious penalties. Most of the
smaller stuff in any place is handled by a more informal process. 

Food for thought: there's no absolute ban on talking in a library, but
the librarians usually have the authority to ask a particular group to
leave. There *is* an absolute ban on smoking in our library, but the
action is the same: asking the person or group to leave.


It's a fuzzy world, sometimes.



Disclaimer: It's late and these are just ponderings, not official
pronouncements. You can quote me if you include this disclaimer.
