> from using this to punish the types of people they dislike ("common
> sense" is not a good answer)?

What do you mean by "complaint"? I haven't heard the word "complaint"
here yet. I thought this discussion centered around the matter of
why you see a "threat" serious enough to muzzle free speech in a
polite request from a system administrator, but not in an explict
declaration that somebody "needs" a rape. To whose complaint do you
refer? 

> 	I think everyone would agree they are wrong, in a moral sense.
> However, what is criminal is another matter entirely. The question is
> not "are threats wrong?", but "what's a *CRIMINAL* threat?".

Well, I have no trouble with that at all. 

A person who threatens rape to another person (including a generic
threat that "someone" will rape the person, or that someone "should"
rape them has committed a criminal offence (at least, they have done
so under the criminal code of Canada).

An administrator who explains policy to a student, or who tells a student
that they will face disciplinary action for refusing to go along with the
policy has commited no offence whatsoever. As far as the law goes, they have
acted properly.

Somehow, I don't think you want to discuss the issue of "criminality".
Neither do I. I do want to discuss the issue of the effect of threats on
regular interaction on the net. And in that context, it seems ludicrous
to me to make a major case of a polite (and perfectly reasonable) request
from a system administrator while brushing of the effect of a direct e-mail
rape threat. 

