the article in question. You've probably already guessed... It's an attack
on the relevance and meaning of Hannah Arendt's judgements about Nazi
morality, based on the allegations (made by the poster - I have no idea
whether they're true) that she slept with a Nazi professor while in school
and that she fled Europe during the war rather than staying to fight the
Germans. All this couched in intermittently vulgar language, and in terms
(obviously) of personal attack on Arendt's character.

Now, I can easily see how someone might find this offensive. But it is
unquestionably relevant to a discussion of Arendt's status as a philosopher,
and not (so far as I can tell) inappropriate given the charter of t.phil.m.
I can only assume that Prof. Ginzberg was more concerned about the Anglo-
Saxon terminology than about the appropriate use of the group, which makes
sense given her earlier references to fear of sexual-harrassment litigation.

All of which may go to show that sometimes it *is* important to hold the line
on censorship issues, because the expression at issue may not be content-
free obscenity after all.
