Karl, you know better than this.  Very, very little of USENET is taxpayer
funded in the sense you're thinking of.  You may be thinking of the NSFNet,
which is taxpayer funded, but which is orthogonal to USENET.

The only parts of USENET which are taxpayer funded are the internal subnets
of government sites.  They must follow whatever rules that implies.

Interestingly enough, the first amendment is most definitely not one of
those rules!  The government publishes all sorts of information, and
only puts in these publications what it wants to.  It has no duty to
provide the means of publication to the people, and has every right to
bar the public from using the means of publication funded by the government.

The U.S. 1st amendment, and other clauses like it, forbid the government
from interfering with the private press.  They do not give guaranteed
access to the public press by the citizens.

Think about it.  In most people's minds, the fact that NSFNet is
goverment funded implies that there is material you *can't* send over
it, not that everybody has the right to send whatever they want!
