In article <24pnen$9gn@crchh327.bnr.ca> ethridge@bnr.ca (Allen Ethridge) writes:
>: And has been discussed here at enormous length, that is a
>: very, very weak point.  I do not post about sex, period.

>As far as i can see, the only thing that's weak about it is that
>you refuse to deal with it.

Grow up.  The original question was something along the
lines of "to what extent is the net a hostile place for
women, and does sexual harassment contribute to it?"  I
had the unmitigated nerve to describe my own experiences.
Kuoppola noted that I was posting to soc.motss and
decided to ask if I was writing about sex.  I answered 
(and answered and answered) that soc.motss is not a 
sex newsgroup (which does not preclude the possibility of
people posting about sex to it, and yes, people have
posted about sex to rec.equestrian, too) and that I simply
do not write about sex on the net.  That seems to have
been insufficient and also seems to have provoked some
hostility.  Big deal.

The problem with this entire discussion is how the
'question' is being framed.  When we persist in framing the
discussion in terms of the behavior of the 'victim' (a word
I detest, but I haven't got anything better), we are
talking about symptoms and not about root causes.
Harassers may be provoked by some behavior on the part of
the harassee, but that's not *why* they harass ("guns don't
shoot people, people shoot people").  Whether or not I post
about sex may be interesting to you in a prurient sort of
way, but even if I did it would not lessen the
responsibility of my readers for their own actions.

Electronic media provide a distancing mechanism for
harassers, which places this vaguely in the same category
as obscene telephone calls.  We never actually see the
person on the other end, and, in the case of forgeries,
we don't even know who that person is.  This apparently
frees some people to do things that they would most likely
not do in person, because they know it's wrong.

A different problem is those who do things they don't
understand are wrong, like those unwanted talk requests
mentioned in another posting.  In both cases I think that
education is a key component in prevention.  Just who
should be responsible for that education, however, I have
no idea.  Simply sticking an antiharassment policy in
campus or corporate computer use guidelines seems
insufficient.
