Ummm.  Overprotectiveness, which I'd always thought was just a
characteristic of us Americans (I am one, I just live here) but which
is moving eastwards as well.  I'm arguing against a block in our centre
who think that we shouldn't make news available to undergrads because
'it is our responsibility to protect them from ways to waste their
time'.  May not sound the same, but some of the issues are similar.

Anyway.  First, let me say that I find your post disturbing.  I'm not
sure whether I am more frightened by your claim that you fear being
sued for simply exposing your students to the real world, or whether
there might not be even more sinister implications in the fact that
you, as an academic, seem to think that such a threat is a reasonable
control upon your studies and teaching.

It is the business of educational establishments to train young people
to function in the real world.  Said real world includes a lot of stupid
people, a bunch of misguided people, and a fair share of just plain
nasty ones.  You wouldn't be doing your students any favors by trying
to hide this so you can pretend it's not the case.  As for parents who
think that you should be censoring everything their kids read, you'd be
doing them, their kids, and all your other students, a big favor by
pointing out to them that if what they really want for their kids is a
babysitter, they could get one a lot cheaper than by sending them off
to school.

It's also the business of educational establishments to promote discussion
of ideas.  Some of the ideas discussed are going to be offensive to some
people.  Indeed, I'd suggest that if a University is unwilling even to
hear such ideas, then it might as well close down.  Rather, it ought to
be actively demonstrating what is 'wrong' with them (from whatever point
of view the University espouses) and why.  Your students will come out
a lot better if they have been exposed to this, and thought about why it
may be 'wrong', than if they have been sheltered from it.  Of course, it
probably makes the course harder to teach, but that's life.

In fact, you should be able to make use of these postings, to discuss
the difference between things which are 'OK', things which are
offensive but it's the listener's problem, things which are
ill-mannered, and things which are arguably 'wrong'.  Indeed, that
opens the whole major philosophical question of 'what ARE right and
wrong?'  And, the question of when tolerance should be exercised, and
to what extent.  Should be just up your alley.

I'm inclined to like (if it makes you more comfortable) the idea that
you warn prospective students that your course may include 'offensive'
material.  (I don't actually see how you can meaningfully DO philosophy
without risking being offensive, but what do I know?)  If you
demonstrate acceptance of limitations, by censoring news groups, then
you are implicitly accepting responsibility for what your students
read, and will some day find that your having your pants sued off by
some parent who doesn't like Witgenstein or who finds Socrates morally
objectionable.  Or, someday the 'New Philosphical Review' (or whatever
philosophers read) will contain an article about evolution.  Or. one of
them will discover Camille Paglia (sp?).

Mildly apropos, I toss a result of my musings about Salman Rushdie's
situation.  It occurs to me that (insofar as heresy and blasphemy are
defined by the various religions) Christianity itself is blasphemous
from the point of view of Muslims and Jews, Islam is blasphemous from
the point of view of Christians and Jews, and Judaism is ditto from the
standpoint of Muslims and Christians.  I would just about bet that,
given any two of us, you could find some belief of one which would
offend the other.

Attempts at censorship are dangerous, and tend to backfire.  The real
danger to freedom is not the nasty outsiders trying to take it away,
but rather the fact that most of us would give up tiny bits of freedom
in areas where we don't expect to exercise it, in return for
restrictions being effective against someone else.  (Maybe the ACLU
could provide you with some background info apropos that idea.)

As for ill-mannered sods, I'm afraid they are just one of the everyday 
hazards of life...
