I don't know how this would be accomplished, since most finger pro-
grams read /etc/passwd, /etc/utmp, /usr/adm/lastlog, and several other
system files.  Has U of I written a "front-end" to (or replacement for)
finger that implements the "unlisted" concept?  If so, I'd *love* to 
take a look at it. 

Admins confronted with similar problems might be interested in the
tcp_wrapper package.  This freeware package allows the admin to make 
decisions about network services (talk, finger, ftp, et al.) based 
on the hostname/domain of the requesting site.  With tcp_wrapper, it 
is possible to say "reject all finger request EXCEPT those from within 
my domain," "reject any ftp requests from host pirate.uploader.edu," 
or even "reject all talk requests from outside my domain, log them, 
and perform a reverse finger of the requesting host."   

The application of tcp_wrapper to an email-bomber or mass-mailer is 
left as an exercise for the reader.  8)

tcp_wrapper should run under most BSD-style network implementations,
as well as some System V environments; it is available via anonymous 
ftp from ftp.win.tue.nl, in the directory /pub/security.

