   >>        Presumably, SRI employees and associates can have logon id's,
   >>        and don't need anonymous ftp. This gets more bizarre by the
   >>        day.
   >  Employees, yes. People they associate with, no, unless you want to give
   >accounts to random people on the internet, just so they can share files
   >with SRI folk.

   You set up a shared guest account for the associates.  If you're
   really security conscious, you filter and drop FTP traffic not from
   sites the associates are known to log in from.

There is a wide range between using FTP to exchange information with a
small fixed set of other people and sites and making it available to
the "universe".  Since people persist in refusing to consider that
such cases could exist, I will go through examples one more time.

1.  A person at the site is the organizer for a conference (eg the
IEEE Fuzzy Logic conference).  The conference announcement tells
people who plan to submit abstracts to pick up the appropriate set of
macros for their text formatter.  Additional info that could not fit
in the announcement is also available.

2. A person at the site is the organizer for a competition at a
conference (eg the AAAI robot competition).  Machine-readable data
needs to be made available to the various contestant as part of the
preparation for the competition.

3. A person at the site has software that is useful to others in his
narrow subfield.  He posts an announcement of its availability to the
appropriate newsgroups.

4. A person has a technical note that invites the interested reader to
pick up either the program or information that would not fit in the
paper.

5. A researcher has expensive hardware and software that can produce
data sets that are useful to others working in the area who do not
have access to such equipment.  He choses to make these data set
available.

Notice that in each of these case, the audience for a file is small
(from a handfull to a few hundred), but the offerer has no way of
knowing who there are (in advance).

So why not be an unrestricted site?
1. System load.  Many sites do NOT have a dedicated FTP server.  For
example, my FTP server also handles multiple large Internet mailing
lists.  Multiple sites sweeping your FTP server can chew up a
substantial portion of the available CPU cycles.  We switched from an
unrestricted to a restricted site because this was happening too
often.

2. Enhanced services for the offerers of files.  Some people who
offered files via FTP liked to know who got them (so they can notify
the recipient of patches, new releases, supplementary material or to
request feedback).  At some sites, the offerer must count of the
recipient to e-mail a message saying that he had gotten the package.
This is unreliable and we had an a script to automatically collect and
forward this information.  However this becomes useless if 80-90% of
the retrievals are by programs that sweep up everything they
encounter.

Admonition: Don't assume that someone else doesn't have a good reason
for doing what they are doing just because it isn't immediately
obvious to you.

-- Doug Moran

