Thus spake jhenders@jonh.wimsey.bc.ca (John Henders): 

>    The Usenet dilemma.
>
	But easily resolved for a change.

	If such a group existed, all that would be necessary would be to
forward the offending mail to the individual it supposedly originated with
along with a brief note stating that the message would be posted to
alt.harassing.email (or whatever). The person receiving it would then have
three choices:

	1. Ignore it and allow it to be posted, thus tacitly admitting that
	   s/he _did_ write it - or at least not denying it.

	2. Respond with a statement that s/he did _not_ write it. If true,
	   this would allow the individual to start an investigation into
	   the question of who was abusing her/his account. If false, it
	   would force the sender to deny his/her reaction - which would
	   rather effectively destroy the impact of his/her message. In 
	   either case, the message could still be posted with a note which
	   stated clearly that the individual named in the address denied
	   writing the message which would discourage his/her future use
	   of email to attack other users since s/he would then be publicly
	   known.

	3. Apologize and hope that the individual s/he attacked was willing
	   to forget the matter and not post the message.

	Any of these responses would have a positive result. If the person
the message supposedly originated with did _not_ send it, this would allow
him/her to do something about the abuse of his/her email address before
something was mailed/posted which got him/her in serious trouble. If the
person _did_ (as is normally the case) send it, s/he would be forced to
either retract the statements (which would both humiliate him/her and
discourage future harassing mailings) or accept the reaction of the Usenet
community to that type of behavior.

	Personally - I would _love_ to see such a group created.



