Actually, the REAL problem is a variant to that which Prof. Ginzberg
(no relation to The Judge :-) pointed out, although she did not state
it explicitly.  We have people demanding and abusing "rights" with
complete and utter disregard for the responsibility that comes along
with those rights.

Because we have largely lost our corporate sense of "right" and
"wrong", people tend to think only it terms of "legal" and "illegal".
Was is legal for someone to post porno articles to the philosophy
newsgroups?  Quite probably.  Was it right?  Definitely not.  It was
an abuse of the PRIVILEGE of using the Net.  And a dedicated minority
of abusers can almost always destroy the privileges of a group, simply
because something like the Usenet cannot function *effectively* if
enough people abuse it.  Even if there is no official attempt to
control the contents of the groups, people will not able to use it and
institutions will not be willing to carry it, both for fears of
litigation and because it is not worth wading through the garbage just
to get to the few worthwhile posts.

>Second, these articles aren't "in your face".  You have to ask for their
>display.  This, at least in my mind, removes the entire discussion from the
>realm of imposition of harassment and takes it into the realm of consensual
>contact.

The CS department from which I graduated spent over a year in
litigation and negotiation over an incident where a female graduate
student found, in a public image directory, an image of a woman
wearing no top.  The overwhelming legal opinion at the time was that
having such pictures on the system without being clearly marked as
offensive created a harassing and hostile work environment.  The
university eventually settled out of court because they knew they
would lose, and the public image directory was removed from the
system.  This was for a completely voluntary, non-compulsory
feature provided as a courtesy to the students.

Because a small group of people abused the privilege of using that
directory by putting inappropriate pictures there, the whole thing was
taken away from us.  I fear that a similar fate could befall Usenet.

-mark

