In article <1993Aug17.190530.8135@camax.com>,
Mark Meuer <meuer@camax.com> wrote:
>
>The CS department from which I graduated spent over a year in
>litigation and negotiation over an incident where a female graduate
>student found, in a public image directory, an image of a woman
>wearing no top.  The overwhelming legal opinion at the time was that
>having such pictures on the system without being clearly marked as
>offensive created a harassing and hostile work environment.  

I'm afraid that I'll have to admit that I think that's just plain
silly, not to mention counterproductive.  There are certainly lots of
ways in which workplaces can (and I believe should) be changed to 
make them less 'harassing and hostile' to various groups, but IMHO
concentrating on such minutiae distracts the compaigners from putting
energy in much larger problems, and creates a public atmosphere of
'that's silly' which makes it harder to press the important cases.

As a case in point, consider the continual renaming of people of
African descent -- whatever they are called now.  When I was very
young, it was considered proper and respectful to refer to them as
'colored people' in preference to any of the other available terms.
Then they became 'negroes', then 'black', then Afro-American, ...

There must be some such on the other side of my screen; how many of you
find that 'white society' treats you more fairly simply because you are
now Afro-Americans rather than colored people?  The naming game has
simply provided an escape route whereby people can pretend to be
thinking about the problem, without actually having to make any changes
to their behaviour.  It wastes the energy of the campaigners, and
provides an easy escape hatch for the 'oppressors'.  The energy would
have been better spent elsewhere.

Similarly, how many wheelchair users out there find that being referred
to as 'mobility disadvantaged' makes it easier for you to get your
wheelchair up the stairs?

>Because a small group of people abused the privilege of using that
>directory by putting inappropriate pictures there, the whole thing was
>taken away from us.  I fear that a similar fate could befall Usenet.

Yes, but what needs resisted is this knee-jerk reaction to the abuse.
There will always be abusers.  You (not you personally, this isn't
meant as an attack, but 'you' plural meaning your institution -- or,
maybe at a stretch, meaning whoever it is that they are afraid of) have
simply put the abusers in control -- precisely the opposite of the
desired effect.  You've now established a precedent under which anyone
who doesn't want some set of ideas discussed need only inject some
offensive item into the discussion forum, and they can cause it to be
closed down.  You are getting your (not meant personally, I remind you)
censorship, but it is being imposed by precisely those people you would
want to censor.  The worst of all worlds.

All in all, I am more comfortable with a world in which people are
permitted to spout drivel than with one in which the 'system' takes it
upon itself to try to regulate it.  In the extreme, you again end up in
a worst of all worlds situation (and you can find examples among the
world's dictatorships) in which you get regulation of speech by the
system, AND information being reduced to a set of drivel being spouted
by said system.

