Sure, why not?  It's early, and I've just stoked up on caffeine for 
the day........8)

	This department provides you with access to various public
	electronic forums.  You enjoy the full range of free speech
	rights when participating in these forums; we have no inten-
	tion of interfering with free speech.  However, we request
	that you consider the feelings and sensibilities of other
	users when participaing; please remember that some material
	may be found offensive, or even harassing, by other users.

	You bear full responsibility for your contributions to these
	forums.  Remember that you are subject to the University's 
	rules and regulations, as well as local, state and Federal
	law.  Should another user file charges against you, those
	charges will be handled within the appropriate due process
	system.  We will not arbitrarily restrict your participation; 
	neither will we attempt to protect you from the legitimate 
	complaints and challenges that may result from your partici-
	pation. 

>  2) *Should* a department express its dislike of legal material
>      in the same policy that actually bans material?

Perhaps.......I think that it would depend on the nature of the site
in question.  I don't believe that a "general purpose" site, carrying
soc/talk/rec/alt groups, should make an official expression of dislike.
A site dedicated to a specific purpose (a computer science or engi-
neering site, perhaps, that only carried comp/sci newsgroups) *might*
be able to get away with it.

It seems to me that the act of providing the forum (newsgroup, that
is, rather than Usenet itself) is an expression of tolerance.  If 
you provide the newsgroups, you shouldn't express official dislike.
Such an official expression would be akin to a restaurant saying, "we
put chalkboards in our bathrooms, but we don't really want you writing
on them."

