The issue isn't "sexual harrassment," but "excessive crossposting."
Ultimately, there isn't a sure-shoe way of preventing it.   Crossposting
in in the technology for good and sufficient reasons.  Unfortunately,
there is no easy way, other than hiring and supporting some modern-day
Anthony Comstock, to prevent abusive use of it.  

(rest of comments deleted)

What you have brought up are a bunch of thorny problems.  If you want to
use a technology, you have to use it within the context of its limits.
Usenet is a wide-open party line technology that lets almost any sort of
abuse take place.  Many sysadmins, particularly in the landgrant.edu
sites, have very sharp limits on what they can and cannot do.  Taking
decisive action can very well mean litigation, bad press, and all sorts
of bad PR.  If you've got a way to shut off the Turkish propaganda and
the holocaust-didn't-happen traffic that will work, every sysadmin on
this group will pay lots of attention.  If you want to know why "we"
haven't come up with the answers, maybe "we" aren't smart enough, and I
think that the "we" I'm talking about isn't a bunch of junior people who
have nothing better to do than march around campuses and shout
obscenities through bullhorns.  Indeed, you might want to consider that
part of what you perceive as a problem was that the freedom to do just
that with bullhorns was fought for and established a generation ago.  

The used of a local moderated newsgroup or listserv technology might
meet your objectives, and I think both of them have been mentioned.  

Having run the gauntlet of what's legal in terms of the law, what can
get me fired, and that sort of thing, a few times, I would suggest that
those issues are Wesleyan issues in your case, and that some discussion
with your colleagues and administration might be in order.  If I were a
Dean, Provost, or President I would certainly welcome a discussion about
what does and does not "go" on "our turf."  But since I am not, I have
no answers for you, and I don't think anyone else here is prepared to
speak meaningfully about Wesleyan's policies.  

As to what you term "red herrings," I think that the "Chronicle of
Higher Education" is chock full of stuff on acceptable speech, sexual
harrassment, and stuff like that, with occasional references to Usenet.

Of course, corrupting youth has been an issue since at least 399 B.C.E.,
when Socrates came out second in a field of one on the topic.
