Here's my stab at a "realpolitik" version:

At this time it is not clear where the right to free speech conflicts
with the right to enjoy a harassment-free workplace. 
We love the idea of providing as much information as possible and 
doing as little censorship as possible.To show respect for your fellow
users of the net, and to keep the peace, we strongly recommend that 
controversial material be kept to those areas where it is expected 
and welcomed. In return, we will fight to make sure that you have free
and uncensored access to these areas, and an uncensored right to have
any legal material in your private directories and private e-mail, and
to continue to run a system with no forbidden uses, and only minimal
resource and priority-based restrictions.

   You have a constitutional right to use us to make a fight based on
either unlimited free speech or unlimited protection from harassment. 
Be aware, though, that this department is understaffed and we would 
rather spend our time working to improve the system than fighting 
lawsuits. Our bosses feel even more strongly about this. This
University also has strong political winds and may react very swiftly
to harassment charges. In other words, if you create a political hot
potato, we could be forced to stop carrying Usenet news on the grounds
that it is making too much work for us unrelated to our duties. This
could also happen if a user creates such a storm that our system is
"mail-bombed" or otherwise made the target for harassment because of
the actions of a single user. 

   So, for everyone's benefit, we ask that our public message of the
day board be kept free of everything except community announcements,
your .plan file be kept clean, and that your posts to Usenet are clean
where clean is called for (ie restrict sex, obscenity, and violent
material to those alt groups which encourage them.) If you break these
guidelines, we are afraid that the result will be that the whole
community will be denied our access to these resources. Please, don't
use us for this battle. We fought to get Usenet news, we fought to get
more disk space for news and for users, we fought to get more machines
and we've fought to keep the news despite limited resources. We've
been fighting to deal with the results of five consecutive years of
budget cuts, there are two of us professional staff trying to support 
45 machines running eight different operating systems, and all we want
is peace and uptime and a chance to work on our long list of system
improvements. Please help us out and think about your actions.


DISCLAIMER: THIS IS NOT a policy, this is just my musing on what a
completely *HONEST* policy statement might look like.  It does reflect
my real feelings but it is too raw to use as an official statement. 
May be anthologized with this disclaimer.



