Subject: NPR story on email privacy
Date: 20 Aug 1993 19:00:42 -0400
Message-ID: <199308202300.AA11789@eff.org>

Today's _All Things Considered_ on National Public Radio featured a story on
the privacy, or alleged lack thereof, of electronic mail.

Unlike most such stories, which focus on employer interception of email, this
piece focused the potential for dissemination, through the Internet, of mess-
ages beyond the audience intended by the sender.  It cited the case of a Yale
professor who was the victim of a bombing, who sent a note to departmental
colleagues assuring them he was on the mend; the note was forwarded by one
recipient to a University publication, from whence it eventually found its
way to the Washington Post.  It quoted one source to the effect that he now
states explicitly when his messages are to be kept confidential.

My subjective impression is that while the potential for problems of this
sort definitely does exist, the story may leave lay listeners with an exagger-
ated impression of the extent to which such things actually happen, as well
as create unnecessary apprehension among some users of the net.

