>I just thought whether there's a differnece - around here when
>dicussing policy the issues have been things like:
>
>1) whether to start using disk quotas, and if so, how should the
>   quotas be set
  Quotas are good if disk space is tight, and you don't have people randomly
needing large amounts of space for data. Of course, there should be some
way to increase quotas, and a common directory for utilities and images would
probably be nice. (GIFs can take up a lot of space. Having a common directory
for them, or a list of available ones and where they're located could save
quite a bit of space.)

>2) whether it is right for the system admininstrators to read
>   people's home directories (email privacy is legally protected here)
  I'd say that administrators should be under the same constraints as other
users, as long as the user's not threatening a machine. (Sending mailbombs,
crashing machines regularly, trying to crack someone else's ID. That sort
of thing.)

>3) what is "OK" by the government's no-commercial-use policy
>   - is one allowed to give hints (by posting to local newsgroups
>   on a machine owned and maintain by the Student Union)
>   on where to buy stuff cheap, and what is advertizing
  As long as the student isn't running a business, or associated with
the business he's reccomended, no problem. ("Garage sales" are OK,
advertising a typing service isn't.)

>4) what to do about possible "crackers" so people acting foolishly
>   (trying to break into U.S. military machines, for example)
>   will not endanger the Finnish Internet connection (I don't
>   think this is any longer a real risk)
  You could lower their quotas, give them padded shells, or kick them off
the machines either temporarily or permanantly.

  That's my off-the-cuff opinions.
