   I recently returned to work after my vacation from the electronic world of
e-mail and newsgroups to find that the machine which I maintenace was marked
as a site for illegal software in lists posted on this newsgroup. (By 
coup@gnu.ai.mit.edu (Christopher Klaus))

   I would first like to say that I am glad that something is being done about
the continuing problem of software piracy on the Internet. Concerning this
machine, however, it has been cleared of all such devices of software piracy
such as FSP and anonymous FTP. It is a graphics modeling workstation, and
is used by few people.

   I will share what did happed, so that perhaps we can circumvent other
SysAdmin's stations from being overrun, and possibly repossessed by the
SPA or FBI. About 5 months ago my boss was contacted by one of the security
consultants from Iowa State about an anonymous tip that this machine was
being used to transfer pirated software using the FSP protocol. At that time,
I had no clue as to what FSP was. The machine was taken off the net, and we
spent many hours searching through it. While nothing that appeared to be
pirate software was found, there was evidence left behind that suggested that
the machine had been assulted by a hacker, and in fact the FSP programs did
exists in a directory on the machine.

   The machine was kept off the internet until it had been wiped down, the
original system software re-installed, and the default passwords/anonymous
logins/uucp connections/etc had been disabled. Since then, we have had no
security breeches.

   A word to the wise: Log EVERYTHING. And keep track of your users. It is
possible on some machines to lock out the use of spare sockets by normal users.
This is a good idea, as that way FSP cannot be started.

   Good luck.

